title: Cisco Nexus: Spanning-Tree Topology Changes (all VLANs)
agents: snmp
catalog: hw/network/cisco
license: GPLv2
distribution: nxos_stp_tcn
description:
 Monitors spanning-tree topology changes of all VLANs of a Cisco Nexus (NX-OS)
 switch in one service. Data is collected by the special agent "Cisco Nexus STP
 topology changes (SNMPv3 VLAN contexts)", which reads the VLAN list from
 CISCO-CONTEXT-MAPPING-MIB and queries the BRIDGE-MIB objects
 dot1dStpTopChanges and dot1dStpTimeSinceTopologyChange in each VLAN's numeric
 SNMPv3 context.

 The service lists every VLAN in its details and records one metric - and so
 one graph - per VLAN: the time since that VLAN's last topology change.

 The service is {CRIT} if any VLAN had a topology change within the configured
 critical window (default: 12 hours) and optionally {WARN} within a longer
 warning window; the summary names the affected VLANs, most recent first. A
 VLAN that never had a topology change is always {OK}, because the NX-OS timer
 then counts from the start of spanning tree (e.g. a reboot) rather than from
 a change.

 Optionally, upper levels can be set on the topology change rate of each VLAN
 (changes per hour), measured between two collections. A decreasing counter
 (reboot, Counter32 wrap) yields no rate for that interval. The special agent
 rule can cache its answer, so a switch with many VLANs is queried at most
 every configured interval instead of on every check.

 VLANs whose SNMP context does not answer are listed and set the service to a
 configurable state (default {UNKNOWN}); all other VLANs are still evaluated.
 The same rule can restrict monitoring to, or exclude, specific VLANs.

 Verified on Nexus 9000 C93240YC-FX2 with NX-OS 10.2(5). NX-OS 10.4(5) on
 C93180YC-FX3 does not provide these BRIDGE-MIB objects via SNMP.

discovery:
 One service is created per switch if at least one VLAN has spanning-tree data.
 VLANs added later appear in the existing service without a new discovery.
